{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of J1 at head f63f0015, for request 7d6ce0f6 and review request d0f33a50. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED. All five earlier items are repaired. One new defect remains: the button's fence lift lets a page send a replacement with no answer. The repair is small, and it changes source, so the next head needs a new gate.\n\n## The change I request\n\n**A press with no answer lifts the fence.** `settledAssociations` in `apps/room/page/src/original-check.ts` excludes only `waiting`, `unknown` and `unverified`. So `gone` and `mismatch` also lift the page's fence, and `original-copy.scope.test.ts:680` asserts that they do. The note's own row says that a press with no answer lifts nothing, and section 9 says that a missing row does not show that no act was sent.\n\nThe path, from reading the code. I did not run it:\n1. The laptop loses a reply, and its page holds the \"outcome unknown\" fence.\n2. The phone presses the button. The act has landed, so the row ends.\n3. The laptop still shows the row, from an older draw or from the last rows of a failed read. Its person presses.\n4. `read` answers `not-found`, so the state is `gone`. The page deletes `lastActs` and clears `unknown`, and the line says \"This request has no open copy.\"\n5. The person can send the comment again, as a new intent with a new entry.\n\nThat second entry is what J1 exists to prevent. A sweep, the time bound or a stolen session's deletion reach the same state.\n\n`mismatch` has the same fault. The send path keeps that answer as a fence (`main.ts:646`), and the button removes it. `archived` is also a fence on the send path, so it should stay.\n\nThe repair: only `landed` and `refused` lift a fence. Change line 680, and add a control that turns it red if `gone` lifts one. The fence then stays until a reload, as it did before this head, when no row remains to press. State that in section 9.\n\n## Your four questions\n\n1. **The bound on requests to membership: good enough. Add no second cap.** A second cap brings back the lockout that the exception removes. Only an admin's first action list holds `membership.invite`, so the rows grow only with members that an admin admits. Cloudflare's limits page states 10 GB per Durable Object, so it takes about 600 active members who each fill 16 MiB on purpose. The same page says that writes then fail with `SQLITE_FULL`, and that `DELETE` still succeeds. I did not test that here, and I did not trace whether the alarm can finish its work at that point. The page also states 1 GB on the Free plan in one section, and I did not check which plan applies. Replace \"nobody measured\" with these figures, labelled as the provider's statement and not a measurement.\n2. **Dropping the rows of a member with no active key: right.** Those rows can wait for a device that may never come, and an active person is stopped now. The journal stays true, and section 9 states the loss.\n3. **The 14 earlier controls: run them on the next head.** A line that still occurs once does not show that a case still turns red, and these cases now read rows through a new helper. The longest control in `controls/first-run/summary.jsonl` took 13 seconds, so the 14 cost about three minutes.\n4. **The reload limit: accepted.** That page behaves as a page did before J1. The sentence is too narrow, though. `heldSessions` is keyed on the `Session` object, and `sessionOf` in `main.ts:116` makes a new one for each open. So the three `opened.clear()` calls in `main.ts` forget the memory too, and the page can then open on signed reads. The comment on `sessionLost` says \"for this room\". Correct both sentences. You can key the memory by service, membership and key if you prefer.\n\n## The five earlier items\n\n1. **Long `kind`: fixed.** The 256-byte bound sits in `keep`, and I rechecked the arithmetic: a row is at most 1,889 bytes by the comment's own terms, under the stated 1,900. `openOriginals` now tells a failed read from an empty list and keeps the last rows, the lock and the button.\n2. **Full object: fixed.** `#sweep` stops at 16 members and 64 rows. It resumes correctly after a break, and it wraps when a pass finishes. The member form of `standingOf` reports `removed` for every state other than active, and `activeKey` from `hasActiveKey`, so a retired key with an active sibling keeps its rows.\n3. **\"Nothing was sent\" with a row open: fixed.** `keepToSend` checks before the keep and ends the row on a stop after it, on both paths of `act` and in the editor. Nothing awaits between that check and `submit`.\n4. **Failed renewal: fixed.** `sessionLost` stops the send, and `renewLost` asks again at the next send or draw.\n5. **The statements: fixed.** The comments and the note now state what a session can make. I read `issueDeviceKey`, and `issueInvitation`'s call in `connection-custody.ts` shows no copy, as section 9 says. I did not read the helper's browser record in `packages/cli`.\n\n## Two points that do not block\n\n- **An older row can lift a newer request's fence.** `Acted` holds no digest, and the lift matches on scope and key. It takes an answered request whose `DELETE` failed, a later send whose reply was lost, and then a press on the older row. Compare the digest, or state this in section 9.\n- **A lost session asks again on each draw.** That is one signed session request per draw for as long as the failure lasts, and the route's rate limit bounds it. No change needed.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n**Architecture.** Sound. The table stays outside history, and the sweep is bounded and reads only the object's own head. The fence's lift belongs with the answers that settle a request, which is the requested change.\n\n**Security.** No authority issue. A session still makes a row only from a request that its key signed, and the sweep drops only rows of members who are not active or who hold no active key. No member can reach another's rows through it. The requested change prevents a duplicate act, not an escalation.\n\n**Simplification.** Acceptable. `renewLost` and `fresh` overlap slightly, but they answer different states. The memory-only `#sweptTo` needs no storage, because a new instance starts at the first member.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- **Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` hashes to `4867110d…`, each listed file verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's head is `57269b5b`, it exited 0, and its log shows 3242 plain and 926 native tests. The diff from there to this head touches only the note and its manifest. The witness and the last controls record `c50c9723`. All 7 of the last controls distinguish. In the first run, 35 of 38 do and 3 are inconclusive, the three that the note names. I read those results and did not rerun them.\n- **Tests I ran at this head.** `original-copy.scope.test.ts` and `retained-editor.scope.test.ts` pass 21 of 21 on the native configuration. `security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 10 with 0 failures. `check-kernel-boundary` reports 127 sources and 0 refusals. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, `check-doc-links` 0 broken links, and `check-commit-messages` 0 flagged. The worktree stayed clean.\n- **Not done.** I reproduced no path above, and I read this repair myself with no independent reader. I did not read the test diff beyond the cases named here, or the changes to `two-devices.mjs`. No Worker, real room or second physical device has run this code.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4353e21bd4b64cdd0e63433809939d4094cc273d","binding":"assigned","head":"f63f0015fa51b900f46ef554aa296a99a02a6e14","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:93e93b9c7463dc863a2f75eb87ccf6fa3e305c35\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3649cf4ac25e995f657730259cbc8b1b415812d9\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:93e93b9c7463dc863a2f75eb87ccf6fa3e305c35","review_path":"reviewguard